⍝ AllPrimitives.tc
⍝ ════════════════

⊣««« ⍝ Function ON_ERROR ARGS: write /tmp/ALL_PRIMITIVES_ERROR_N.apl:
 a self-contained script that reconstructs A, B, X (via 10 ⎕CR)
 and then executes OPER, so the failure captured in
 ARGS←(N A B X OPER KIND) can be reproduced later. KIND (from
 ONE_PRIMITIVE/DESCRIBE_ERR) is a nested vector of one or two comment
 lines identifying what kind of failure this is -- e.g. "EXECUTE: RANK
 ERROR", "GENERATE B: DOMAIN ERROR" (a bad rval.def stim, not the
 primitive itself), "CONSTRAINT: SYNTAX ERROR" (a bug in the table's
 Z_constraint expression), or "POSTCONDITION FAILED" (OPER ran fine,
 but Z fails the postcondition -- the most common and most interesting
 case).
 A genuine interpreter crash (assertion failure, segfault) can never
 reach here: the process dies before ON_ERROR runs, so there is no
 reproducer for those -- see the crash dump on stderr / core file
 instead.
»»»

∇ON_ERROR ARGS;N;A;B;X;OPER;KIND;FH;LINES;NAME;FMT;TSTR
 (N A B X OPER KIND)←ARGS
 NAME←'/tmp/ALL_PRIMITIVES_ERROR_%d.apl' ⎕FIO.sprintf N
 FH←'w' ⎕FIO.fopen NAME
 FMT←'%s',(⎕UCS 10)
 ⊣{⊣((⊂FMT),⊂('⍝ ',⍵)) ⎕FIO.fprintf FH}¨KIND
 TSTR←'⍝ %d-%d-%d %d:%d:%d' ⎕FIO.sprintf 6↑⎕TS
 ⊣((⊂FMT),⊂TSTR) ⎕FIO.fprintf FH
 LINES←(10⎕CR 'A'),(10⎕CR 'B'),(10⎕CR 'X'),⊂OPER
 ⊣{⊣((⊂FMT),⊂⍵) ⎕FIO.fprintf FH}¨LINES
 ⊣⎕FIO.fclose FH
∇

⊣««« ⍝ Function DUMP_LASTCALL A B X OPER: unconditionally overwrite
 /tmp/ALL_PRIMITIVES_LASTCALL.apl with a reproducer for the call about
 to be made, BEFORE executing it. A genuine interpreter crash (segfault,
 fatal assertion) kills the process before ON_ERROR ever runs, so
 without this there is no way to recover what inputs caused it -- this
 is the only reproducer a crash (as opposed to a caught error or a
 postcondition violation) leaves behind. Overwritten every call, so it
 always holds the LAST attempted call, i.e. the one that just crashed
 the process if the process is no longer running.
»»»

∇DUMP_LASTCALL ARGS;A;B;X;OPER;FH;LINES;FMT
 (A B X OPER)←ARGS
 FH←'w' ⎕FIO.fopen '/tmp/ALL_PRIMITIVES_LASTCALL.apl'
 FMT←'%s',(⎕UCS 10)
 LINES←(10⎕CR 'A'),(10⎕CR 'B'),(10⎕CR 'X'),⊂OPER
 ⊣{⊣((⊂FMT),⊂⍵) ⎕FIO.fprintf FH}¨LINES
 ⊣⎕FIO.fclose FH
∇

⊣««« ⍝ Function Z←ALL_PRIMITIVES: run for CONTEXT.SECONDS seconds (or, in
 single-arity mode, for a fixed iteration count), repeatedly calling
 ONE_PRIMITIVE and accounting for successes vs. violations/failures.

 Single-arity mode: pass ++ARITY '<arity string>' on the apl command
 line (GNU APL's "ignored APL application option, 1 argument"
 convention, see apl --help) to restrict CONTEXT.PRIM to exactly one
 rval.def row -- e.g. --eval "'0 ⎕RVAL..." isn't needed, just:

   apl -s --script testcases/AllPrimitives.tc --OFF ++ARITY 'Z←A+B'

 Picking uniformly at random across ~60 rows wastes time when hunting
 down which SPECIFIC primitives still have improper/incomplete
 preconditions: with only ~1/60th of trials landing on the row you
 care about, thousands of iterations are needed for a confident
 per-primitive read. In this mode CONTEXT.SECONDS is ignored in favor
 of a fixed count (++COUNT '<n>', default ITER_DEFAULT below), the
 ⎕DL 0.1 rate limit is skipped (nothing else on the box to protect
 against), and a per-failure-KIND tally is printed via ⎕← at the end
 -- exactly the "where do we stand" answer, instead of just the first
 10 reproducer files.
»»»

∇Z←ALL_PRIMITIVES; ⎕IO;T0;LCNT;ECNT;PREV_BUDGET;PREV_TD;R;ARGV;IDX;ARITY;COUNT;FOCUSED;KINDS;KCOUNT;K;KIDX;SUCC;I;ITER_DEFAULT;ALLARITY;MAX_DUMPS;DUMPALL
 ⊣««« ⍝ 0 ⎕RVAL B requires B to be a rank-1 byte vector (length 0, 8, 16,
  ..., 256), hence the (8⍴256)⊤ encoding -- plain 0 12 31 24 60 60
  1000⊥⎕TS alone would collapse to a scalar and fail with RANK ERROR.
 »»»

 ⊣0 ⎕RVAL (8⍴256)⊤0 12 31 24 60 60 1000⊥⎕TS   ⍝ seed from current time
 ⊣««« ⍝ 5⎕RVAL / 3⎕RVAL change ⎕RVAL's global generator state, which
  outlives this function -- remember the previous settings so they
  can be restored before returning, or every ⎕RVAL call made by
  unrelated code for the rest of the session would silently inherit
  this fuzzer's budget/type mix (that leak once broke Domino_stress.tc
  and Macros.tc, which assume ⎕RVAL's default settings).
 »»»

 ⍝ parse ++ARITY / ++COUNT / ++BUDGET from the command line (see header
 ⍝ comment) BEFORE applying CONTEXT.BUDGET below, so single-arity mode
 ⍝ can default to a much smaller ⍴∈ limit than the ~10000 used for the
 ⍝ broad random sweep: with a real failure rate, ON_ERROR's reproducer
 ⍝ dumps (10⎕CR of A/B, potentially deeply nested) get very expensive
 ⍝ at budget 10000 -- multiply that by up to 10 dumps and a few hundred
 ⍝ iterations, and a single-arity run that should take a second can
 ⍝ take minutes. Small arrays are entirely sufficient for finding
 ⍝ precondition bugs (found via this very budget being too large: it's
 ⍝ what made the ⎕ARG sweep for this tool time out during development).

 ITER_DEFAULT←1000
 ARGV←⎕ARG ◊ ARITY←⍬ ◊ COUNT←ITER_DEFAULT
 IDX←ARGV⍳⊂'++ARITY'
 →(IDX>≢ARGV)/NOARITY
 ARITY←(IDX+1)⊃ARGV
NOARITY:
 IDX←ARGV⍳⊂'++COUNT'
 →(IDX>≢ARGV)/NOCOUNT
 COUNT←⍎(IDX+1)⊃ARGV
NOCOUNT:
 FOCUSED←0<≢ARITY
 →(~FOCUSED)/NOBUDGETOVERRIDE
 CONTEXT.BUDGET←200
 IDX←ARGV⍳⊂'++BUDGET'
 →(IDX>≢ARGV)/NOBUDGETOVERRIDE
 CONTEXT.BUDGET←⍎(IDX+1)⊃ARGV
NOBUDGETOVERRIDE:
 ⍝ ++DUMPALL: unconditionally write /tmp/ALL_PRIMITIVES_LASTCALL.apl
 ⍝ before every OPER call (see DUMP_LASTCALL), so a crash that kills
 ⍝ the process before ON_ERROR can run still leaves a reproducer behind.
 ⍝ Off by default -- doing this on every call, not just failures, is
 ⍝ noticeably slower (an extra 10⎕CR reconstruction per iteration), so
 ⍝ only pay for it when actually hunting a crash.
 DUMPALL←(ARGV⍳⊂'++DUMPALL')≤≢ARGV

 PREV_BUDGET←5 ⎕RVAL CONTEXT.BUDGET   ⍝ apply the ⍴∈ limit
 PREV_TD←3 ⎕RVAL CONTEXT.TD           ⍝ apply the type distribution
 CONTEXT.PRIM←6 ⎕RVAL ⍬
 MAX_DUMPS←10-7×FOCUSED               ⍝ 10 normally, 3 in single-arity mode

 →(~FOCUSED)/NOFOCUS
 CONTEXT.PRIM←(((CONTEXT.PRIM)[;1])ϵ⊂ARITY)⌿CONTEXT.PRIM
 →(0<(⍴CONTEXT.PRIM)[1])/HAVEROWS
 ⎕←'++ARITY ''',ARITY,''' matches no rval.def row. Available arities:'
 ALLARITY←6 ⎕RVAL ⍬
 ALLARITY←(ALLARITY)[;1]
 I←0
ALOOP:
 I←I+1
 →(I>≢ALLARITY)/ADONE
 ⎕←I⊃ALLARITY
 →ALOOP
ADONE:
 Z←¯1 ◊ →RESTORE
HAVEROWS:
NOFOCUS:

 T0←24 60 60⊥⎕TS[4 5 6]
 LCNT←0              ⍝ number of loop iterations so far
 ECNT←0              ⍝ number of ON_ERROR dumps so far
 SUCC←0 ◊ KINDS←⍬ ◊ KCOUNT←⍬   ⍝ per-KIND tally, only used when FOCUSED
LOOP:
 ⍝ checked once per iteration here (not just on the violation path
 ⍝ below), so the time budget/count is enforced regardless of outcome.

 →FOCUSED/CHECK_COUNT
 →((24 60 60⊥⎕TS[4 5 6]) ≥ T0 + CONTEXT.SECONDS)/TIMEOUT
 →AFTERCHECK
CHECK_COUNT:
 →(LCNT≥COUNT)/TIMEOUT
AFTERCHECK:
 LCNT←LCNT+1
 →FOCUSED/SKIPDELAY
 ⊣⎕DL 0.1   ⍝ rate limit (unconditional: caps load on server72 every iteration)
SKIPDELAY:
 ⍝ R.OK, and (if R.OK is 0) R.A R.B R.X R.OPER R.KIND

 R←ONE_PRIMITIVE
 →(~R.OK)/ISFAIL
 SUCC←SUCC+1
 →LOOP                        ⍝ invariant holds: keep fuzzing
ISFAIL:
 →(~FOCUSED)/SKIPTALLY
 K←⊃(R.KIND)[1]                ⍝ first line only, for the tally key
 KIDX←KINDS⍳⊂K
 →(KIDX≤≢KINDS)/HAVEKIDX
 KINDS←KINDS,⊂K ◊ KCOUNT←KCOUNT,0
HAVEKIDX:
 KCOUNT[KIDX]←KCOUNT[KIDX]+1
SKIPTALLY:
 ⊣««« ⍝ ON_ERROR needs the incremented count before the branch decides
  whether to keep going, so it takes its own (ECNT+1) rather than
  sharing the assignment folded into the branch below.
 »»»

 ⍝ dump a reproducer for each of the first MAX_DUMPS errors. In FOCUSED
 ⍝ mode this must NOT stop the loop early -- COUNT is a fixed sample
 ⍝ size for a reliable per-primitive success rate, not a "stop after
 ⍝ the Nth failure" budget (that was fine for the original random-scan
 ⍝ use case, where any 10 failures are equally informative, but here a
 ⍝ handful of early failures would truncate the run to a tiny, biased
 ⍝ sample instead of the requested COUNT attempts).

 →(ECNT<MAX_DUMPS)/DODUMP
 →FOCUSED/LOOP
 →SKIPDUMP
DODUMP:
 ON_ERROR (ECNT+1) R.A R.B R.X R.OPER R.KIND
 ECNT←ECNT+1
 →FOCUSED/LOOP
 →(ECNT<MAX_DUMPS)/LOOP
SKIPDUMP:
 Z←0 ◊ →RESTORE
TIMEOUT:
 Z←1
RESTORE:
 →(~FOCUSED)/NOSUMMARY
 ⎕←''
 ⎕←'=== ++ARITY ''',ARITY,''': ',(⍕LCNT),' attempts, ',(⍕SUCC),' ok, ',(⍕LCNT-SUCC),' failed ==='
 I←0
PLOOP:
 I←I+1
 →(I>≢KINDS)/NOSUMMARY
 ⎕←(⍕I⊃KCOUNT),' x  ',I⊃KINDS
 →PLOOP
NOSUMMARY:
 ⍝ undo the global ⎕RVAL state change from CONTEXT.BUDGET/CONTEXT.TD
 ⍝ above, so the rest of the session sees ⎕RVAL's usual defaults again.

 ⊣5 ⎕RVAL PREV_BUDGET
 ⊣3 ⎕RVAL PREV_TD
∇

⊣««« ⍝ Function ZZ←ONE_PRIMITIVE: run one fuzzing iteration: pick a
 random primitive row from CONTEXT.PRIM (the ⎕RVAL fuzz table),
 generate A/B/X for it, execute it, and check its invariant.

 Returns a structured ZZ (a GNU APL extension): ZZ.OK is 1 if the
 invariant held, 0 if it was violated or if generation/execution
 itself failed; ZZ.A ZZ.B ZZ.X ZZ.OPER are the exact values used,
 for ON_ERROR's reproducer dump when ZZ.OK is 0.
»»»

⊣««« ⍝ Function Z←STAGE DESCRIBE_ERR (RC RES): turn a ⎕EC-style (RC RES)
 pair -- RC=0 means TOK_ERROR, RES is then the 3-or-4-row char matrix
 "like ⎕EM" that ⎕EC returns for it (row 1: error name, optionally
 suffixed '+' when )MORE has content; rows 2-3: offending statement and
 caret; row 4, only present since the QuadFunction.cc ⎕EC fix: the
 )MORE detail itself, previously discarded entirely) -- into a nested
 vector of one or two trimmed text lines prefixed with STAGE, suitable
 for writing as ⍝-comment lines in a reproducer script.
»»»

 ⊣««« ⍝ TRIM: standard trailing-space-trim idiom. An earlier version of
  this comment reported ⌽ on a boolean vector as unreliable (suspected
  packed-BOOL ravel handling in Bif_ROTATE::reverse / Value::try_implode)
  and had TRIM avoid ⌽ entirely via ⍸/⌈//↑ instead. That was traced to
  Value::try_implode()'s single-pass validation, which could corrupt
  ravel.cells[0] before discovering a later non-boolean cell -- already
  fixed (Bugs18 #11, now two-pass). Re-verified: ~3.8M targeted
  iterations of {(⌽∨\⌽⍵≠' ')/⍵} plus a full instrumented AllPrimitives
  fuzz run (comparing both forms on every TRIM call) found zero
  mismatches, so reverted to the plain idiom.
 »»»

∇Z←TRIM S
 Z←(⌽∨\⌽S≠' ')/S
∇

∇Z←STAGE DESCRIBE_ERR ARGS;RC;RES;R1;R4
 (RC RES)←ARGS
 →(RC=0)/ISERROR
 Z←,⊂STAGE,': unexpected ⎕EC result code ',⍕RC
 →0
ISERROR:
 R1←TRIM RES[1;]
 Z←,⊂STAGE,': ',R1
 →((1↑⍴RES)<4)/0
 R4←TRIM RES[4;]
 Z←Z,⊂R4
∇

∇ZZ←ONE_PRIMITIVE;ROW;OPER;EXPD;RC;ET;RES;RC2;ET2;RES2;RCG;ETG;RESG;A;B;X;Z;KIND;DENSEB;RETRY
 ⊣««« ⍝ Z is deliberately declared local here even though it's never read:
  every row's OPER string is literally "Z←...", e.g. "Z←A+B" (baked
  into the ⎕RVAL fuzz table). Without a local Z, that assignment
  would reach the caller's own Z through dynamic scoping instead --
  and since the result variable here is ZZ rather than Z, the
  caller in question is ALL_PRIMITIVES, whose own result IS named Z.
 »»»

 ⊣««« ⍝ B is generated first; A_stim="~B" (set for rval.def rows whose Z
  constraint requires (⍴A)⌈⍴B, i.e. scalar-extension pairs like A+B)
  then calls 7 ⎕RVAL B to conform A to B (scalar or exactly ⍴B) instead
  of generating A independently and hoping it happens to fit.
  TODO: many other primitives (replicate A/B, reshape A⍴B, encode/decode
  A⊤B/A⊥B, catenate, ...) still need their own per-primitive conforming
  rule in ⎕RVAL; for now they keep generating A independently.
 »»»

 ⍝ pick a random primitive: arity A_stim B_stim X_stim Z_constraint

 ROW←(CONTEXT.PRIM)[?≢CONTEXT.PRIM;]
 ⊣««« ⍝ rval.def's ecount column is the literal placeholder token "RE"
  (see rval.def's own header comment: "the APL test runner substitutes
  a concrete value... before calling ⎕RVAL"); replace it here with
  CONTEXT.BUDGET before ⍎'ing A_stim/B_stim/X_stim below, or every
  single generation call fails with VALUE ERROR (RE is not an APL
  name) -- which is exactly what happened before this fix: 100% of
  fuzz iterations silently counted as "violations".

  "SB" (Sqrt Budget) is a second such placeholder, for rows whose
  result size is the PRODUCT of two independently-generated operand
  sizes (A∘.fB: (×/⍴A)×(×/⍴B) elements) -- capping each operand at the
  full CONTEXT.BUDGET still allows BUDGET² elements, which reliably
  timed out the fuzzer. ⌊/⍎/⍕ can't be used unquoted inside rval.def's
  B_stim/A_stim/X_stim fields (they get pasted into a C preprocessor
  macro argument there, and GCC rejects those specific glyphs as
  "not valid in an identifier" -- ¯ is the only APL glyph that macro
  context tolerates unquoted); computing ⌊√CONTEXT.BUDGET⌋ out here
  instead, in already-interpreted APL text, sidesteps that entirely.
 »»»

 ROW[2 3 4]←{c←('RE'≡¯2↑⍵)∧2≤≢⍵ ⋄ s←('SB'≡¯2↑⍵)∧2≤≢⍵ ⋄ (1+c+2×s)⊃⍵ ((¯2↓⍵),⍕CONTEXT.BUDGET) ((¯2↓⍵),⍕⌊0.5+CONTEXT.BUDGET*0.5)}¨ROW[2 3 4]
 ⍝ arity expression (e.g. Z←A+B) and Z constraint, set first so
 ⍝ the caller always has a valid OPER even if generation fails below

 (OPER EXPD)←ROW[1 5]
 ⊣««« ⍝ operator-taking primitives (f/,f¨,f.g,...): substitute a real
  function for the placeholder(s) (?N already respects ⎕IO —
  no +1 needed)
 »»»

 OPER[⍸OPER='f']←(CONTEXT.FPOOL)[?(+/OPER='f')⍴5]
 OPER[⍸OPER='g']←(CONTEXT.FPOOL)[?(+/OPER='g')⍴5]
 A←⍬ ◊ B←⍬ ◊ X←⍬             ⍝ safe defaults in case generation fails below
 ⊣««« ⍝ B is generated first, so that A (and X) can conform to B's ACTUAL
  shape/rank instead of being generated independently and hoping they
  happen to fit (per SVN r2037 discussion: fix this inside ⎕RVAL, not
  by post-hoc patching in this function).
 »»»

 ⊣««« ⍝ Indexing/pick primitives (A⌷B, A⍋B, A⍒B, A⊃B, B[A] and the
  multi-axis B[...] forms) have NO valid index/pick whenever B has a
  genuinely zero-length axis -- not a real primitive defect, just an
  unavoidable consequence of B_stim's shape spec drawing each axis
  length uniformly from 0..N-1, occasionally landing on 0 (see
  fuzzer_findings/rare_errors/pass2_remaining_domain_errors.md).
  Retry B's generation (bounded, to guarantee termination even in a
  pathological case) until it has no zero-length axis, but ONLY for
  these specific arities -- other rows may legitimately want to
  exercise a zero-length-axis B (reshape, catenate, take/drop, ...),
  so this must not change generation for the table as a whole.
 »»»
DENSEB←'Z←A⌷B' 'Z←A⍋B' 'Z←A⍒B' 'Z←A⊃B' 'Z←B[A]' 'Z←B[⊃A[1];⊃A[2]]' 'Z←B[⊃A[1];⊃A[2];⊃A[3]]'
 RETRY←0
GENB_RETRY:
 (RCG ETG RESG)←⎕EC '{⎕RVAL⍣(0<≢⍵)⊢⍵}⍎3⊃ROW'
 →(RCG=1)/GENB_OK        ⍝ generation itself failed (e.g. a bad rval.def stim)
 KIND←'GENERATE B' DESCRIBE_ERR RCG RESG ◊ →FAIL
GENB_OK:
 RETRY←RETRY+1
 →(((⊂OPER)∊DENSEB)∧(0∈⍴RESG)∧RETRY<20)/GENB_RETRY
 B←RESG

 ⊣««« ⍝ Z←⍎B feeds B to the tokenizer as raw, unquoted source text.
  ⎕AV's role since APL1 has always been "the valid characters outside
  string literals" -- originally that meant all 8-bit characters,
  since that was all there was; GNU APL's Unicode support only added
  arbitrary-codepoint characters *inside* string literals, it never
  extended what ⎕AV itself means (confirmed: ⍎"'X'" with X outside
  ⎕AV succeeds, bare ⍎"X" does not). B's unrestricted random-Unicode
  generation (rval.def's plain char type distribution, ~0x0000-0x2FFF)
  almost always lands outside ⎕AV, so this row nearly always just
  re-tests the same boring "not in ⎕AV" rejection instead of
  exercising genuinely interesting ⍎ territory (SYNTAX/VALUE errors
  from malformed-but-tokenizable input, or successful execution).
  Remap every generated element into ⎕AV so it always is one.
 »»»
 →(~(⊂OPER)≡⊂'Z←⍎B')/NOREMAPB
 B←(⍴B)⍴⎕AV[⎕IO+(≢⎕AV)|⎕UCS,B]
NOREMAPB:

 ⊣««« ⍝ X is derived BEFORE A (moved here from after A -- was originally
  generated last), so that markers needing to conform A to B's axis X
  specifically (as opposed to always assuming the last axis) can see
  the real X. Without this, ~BC/~BE (A/B, A\B, A⌿B, ... and their
  [X] siblings) had no way to know which axis the [X] form would
Bad edit op 'X' in line [105]
??? Bad ∇-command
  ERROR otherwise (found via a real fuzzer sweep: A/[X]B, A\[X]B,
  A⊂[X]B all showed ~30% LENGTH ERROR from exactly this).
  X_stim is still generated independently of B, with no idea what B's
  actual rank turned out to be — that produced axis values with no
  relation to B at all (observed: values like 7509335927660159858,
  causing WS FULL in e.g. ⊃[X]B). Every axis-taking primitive in
  rval.def is [X]B, so derive a valid axis from B's real rank instead,
  when one is wanted (X_stim non-empty) and B actually has axes.
 »»»

 (RCG ETG RESG)←⎕EC '{⎕RVAL⍣(0<≢⍵)⊢⍵}⍎4⊃ROW'
 →(RCG=1)/GENX_OK
 KIND←'GENERATE X' DESCRIBE_ERR RCG RESG ◊ →FAIL
GENX_OK:
 X←RESG
 ⊣««« ⍝ Whether an axis is wanted at all must be decided from the X_stim
  SPEC, not from whether the GENERATED X happened to come out empty.
  X_stim's own spec allows rank 0..1 with shape 0..3 (e.g. "¯1 ¯3
  ..."), so a rank-1-length-0 draw is a perfectly ordinary outcome for
  rows that DO want an axis -- checking (0=≢X) here left X as ⍬ on
  exactly that draw, silently skipping the axis substitution below for
  a primitive whose arity requires one (e.g. A↑[X]B), producing a
  bracket index of ⍬ downstream and a guaranteed LENGTH ERROR (found
  via a real fuzzer sweep).
  The obvious-looking (0=≢4⊃ROW) is ALSO wrong, but for a subtler
  reason: rval.def's "empty X_stim stands for ⍬" convention (its own
  header comment) is implemented by storing the literal 2-character
  TEXT "⍬" as the spec for a no-axis row (since ⍬ cannot appear
  unquoted in the C preprocessor) -- so 4⊃ROW is NEVER truly empty; a
  no-axis row's spec has tally 1 (one character, the zilde glyph
  itself), same as a real spec would if it were one character long.
  ≡'⍬' against that spec string is the correct emptiness test; ⍎'ing
  it is what actually produces a genuine ⍬ at runtime (see NORMALA's
  handling of 2⊃ROW for A_stim, which relies on exactly this same fact
  for monadic rows).
 »»»
 →((,'⍬')≡4⊃ROW)/NOAXIS
 →(0=≢⍴B)/NOAXIS
 X←?≢⍴B
NOAXIS:

 →(~(2⊃ROW)≡'~B')/NOTPLAINB
 (RCG ETG RESG)←⎕EC '7 ⎕RVAL B'   ⍝ A_stim="~B": conform to B (scalar or ⍴B)
 →GOTA
NOTPLAINB:
 ⍝ any other ~xxx marker (~BL, ~BF, ~S, ...; see CONFORM_A) also
 ⍝ conforms A to B, just with a different rule than plain ~B. Checked
 ⍝ by leading '~' rather than requiring the CONFORM_A-marker name to
 ⍝ start with "~B" specifically -- ~S (always scalar) doesn't.
 →(~(0<≢2⊃ROW)∧((,'~')≡1↑2⊃ROW))/NORMALA
 (RCG ETG RESG)←⎕EC '(2⊃ROW) CONFORM_A B'
 →GOTA
NORMALA:
 ⍝ still independent for other primitives

 (RCG ETG RESG)←⎕EC '{⎕RVAL⍣(0<≢⍵)⊢⍵}⍎2⊃ROW'
GOTA:
 →(RCG=1)/GENA_OK
 KIND←'GENERATE A' DESCRIBE_ERR RCG RESG ◊ →FAIL
GENA_OK:
 A←RESG
 ⍝ execute OPER; on success (RC=2) RES is the value OPER returned

 →(~DUMPALL)/NODUMPALL
 DUMP_LASTCALL A B X OPER
NODUMPALL:
 (RC ET RES)←⎕EC OPER
 →(RC=2)/EXEC_OK         ⍝ OPER itself failed (e.g. non-conforming random A/B)
 KIND←'EXECUTE' DESCRIBE_ERR RC RES ◊ →FAIL
EXEC_OK:
 ⍝ evaluate the invariant; a plain expr gives RC=1 on success

 (RC2 ET2 RES2)←⎕EC EXPD
 →(RC2=1)/EVAL_OK        ⍝ the constraint expression itself failed to evaluate
 KIND←'CONSTRAINT' DESCRIBE_ERR RC2 RES2 ◊ →FAIL
EVAL_OK:
 ⍝ invariant held (1) or was violated (0)

 KIND←,⊂'POSTCONDITION FAILED'   ⍝ OPER ran fine, but its result fails the postcondition
 ZZ.OK←RES2
 ZZ.A←A ◊ ZZ.B←B ◊ ZZ.X←X ◊ ZZ.OPER←OPER ◊ ZZ.KIND←KIND
 →0
FAIL:
 ZZ.OK←0
 ZZ.A←A ◊ ZZ.B←B ◊ ZZ.X←X ◊ ZZ.OPER←OPER ◊ ZZ.KIND←KIND
∇

⊣««« ⍝ Function Z←MARKER CONFORM_A B: generate a value A that structurally
 conforms to B per the rule named by MARKER, one of:
   ~BL  same rank as B, all axes but the Last one matching B, or scalar
   ~BF  same rank as B, all axes but the First one matching B, or scalar
   ~BC  rank-1 non-negative-int vector, length = B's last axis (for
        A/B, A⊂B: the Count/boundary vector must match B's last axis
        exactly, or LENGTH ERROR). Approximation for the [X] variants
        too: X is derived from B's rank only *after* A is generated
        (see ONE_PRIMITIVE), so the actual axis isn't known yet --
        conforming to the last axis is right whenever X turns out to
        be the last axis, and wrong (LENGTH ERROR, same as before this
        fix) otherwise.
   ~BE  boolean vector for A\B (expand): unlike A/B, values must be
        strictly 0/1 (DOMAIN ERROR otherwise) and it's the *count* of
        1s, not the total length, that must equal B's last axis; the
        rest are zero-fill positions, so total length is >= that.
   ~BR  rank-1 int vector, length = ⍴⍴B exactly (A↓B: one drop-count
        per axis of B; magnitude is irrelevant -- drop clamps, so any
        int works), or scalar.
   ~BD  same rank as B minus 1, shape = B's leading axes (i.e. ⍴B with
        the last axis dropped entirely, not just varied -- unlike
        ~BL); or scalar. For A⌽B: one rotate-amount per row, magnitude
        irrelevant since rotate wraps around.
   ~BDF same as ~BD but drops B's *first* axis instead (for A⊖B, which
        rotates along the first axis: one amount per column).
   ~S   always scalar (int). For A⌽[X]B: a vector A would need to match
        B's shape minus axis X specifically, but X is derived only
        *after* A is generated (see ONE_PRIMITIVE) -- scalar A always
        works regardless of which axis X turns out to be, so it's used
        unconditionally here rather than only as one of two outcomes.
   ~BP  permutation-like: length ⍴⍴B, each value independently 1..⍴⍴B
        (for A⍉B: repeats are legal -- generalized/diagonal transpose
        -- so this isn't a strict permutation, just right-length and
        in-range). ⍬ if B is scalar.
   ~BI  index vector for A⌷B: length ⍴⍴B, element i independently
        1..(⍴B)[i] (a valid index into B's i-th axis specifically, not
        just any value 1..⍴⍴B like ~BP). ⍬ if B is scalar.
   ~BCF like ~BC but conforms to B's *first* axis instead of last (for
        A⌿B, replicate-along-first-axis).
   ~BEF like ~BE but conforms to B's *first* axis instead of last (for
        A⍀B, expand-along-first-axis).
   ~BDEC decode (A⊥B): A's *last* axis must equal B's *first* axis (or
        A scalar); generates A as a vector of exactly that length
        (under-tests the general case where A has other leading axes
        too, but is always structurally valid).
   ~BMM  matrix-multiply-style (Af.gB, inner product): A and B are
        both rank-2 (per B_stim/A_stim already fixing rank=2); A's
        column count (last axis) must equal B's row count (first
        axis). Generates A with a fresh random row count and column
        count = B's row count.
   ~BDOM matrix divide (A⌹B, DOMINO): B is rank-2 (per B_stim already
        fixing rank=2); A's *first* axis (row count) must equal B's
        first axis, A itself rank 1 (vector) or rank 2 (matrix), with
        any column count when rank 2.
   ~FMT  dyadic format (A⍕B): A must have length 1 or 2 and be
        non-negative (LENGTH/DOMAIN ERROR otherwise); generates a
        1-or-2-element vector of small non-negative ints.
   ~BIDX bracket index (A[B]): B (generated first, the array) must be
        rank 1 (RANK ERROR on a matrix); A (the index) can be any
        shape, values must be valid indices 1..≢B.
   ~BPICK pick (A⊃B): B rank 0 (scalar) needs A=⍬; B rank 1 needs A a
        plain scalar index 1..≢B; B rank≥2 needs A=⊂ of an
        (⍴⍴B)-element vector, element i forced into 1..(⍴B)[i]
        (confirmed directly: a flat, non-enclosed multi-element A into
        a matrix B is RANK ERROR, must be enclosed as one index-path).
   ~BIJ  multi-axis bracket index (B[I;J], B[I;J;K]): B's rank fixes
        the number of semicolon-separated axes (2 or 3, via B_stim).
        rval.def's arity string decomposes A itself into per-axis
        index vectors -- "Z←B[⊃A[1];⊃A[2]]" etc -- rather than
        needing ONE_PRIMITIVE to generate separate I/J/K variables
        (it only ever generates A/B/X). A is generated here as an
        (⍴⍴B)-element NESTED vector; element i is itself a vector of
        random length 0..10 (an empty index vector is legal -- B[⍬;J]
        selects a 0-row slice, confirmed directly), values forced into
        1..(⍴B)[i]. Z_constraint then checks ⍴Z against (≢⊃A[1]),
        (≢⊃A[2]) etc, matching B[I;J]'s real shape rule (confirmed
        directly: ⍴(B[I;J]) ≡ (≢I),(≢J), independent per-axis lengths,
        not requiring ≢I=≢J or any relation to B's own shape).
   ~SCR shape-count vector for A↑B: rank-1 int, length = ⍴⍴B exactly
        (LENGTH ERROR otherwise -- A↑B, unlike A⍴B, requires (≢,A)≡⍴⍴B).
        Split off from ~SC (Bugs8 #6, Blake McBride): ~SC generates A
        independent of B's shape, which is correct for A⍴B (any shape
        is legal) but was wrong for A↑B, burning a third of the
        fuzzer's error budget on guaranteed LENGTH ERRORs. Modeled on
        ~BR/RANKVARY (A↓B's marker, same ⍴⍴B-length rule) with ~BD's
        1000| magnitude cap (huge take counts can hang/crash, same
        reasoning as the ~SC history below).
   ~SC  shape/take-count vector for A⍴B: rank-1, independent of
        B, length 0..8 (matching the original raw spec these rows used
        before this marker existed). Generated by asking ⎕RVAL for a
        THROWAWAY array's shape rather than 8 independent magnitude-
        capped ints: ⎕RVAL already clips a generated array's own shape
        internally so ×/shape ≤ CONTEXT.BUDGET (Quad_RVAL.cc's
        do_eval_B), and since ×/R ≥ ⌈/R for non-negative R, that
        product bound also bounds every individual axis length -- no
        separate per-element cap needed, and the guarantee is tied to
        the same budget the rest of the fuzzer already respects rather
        than an arbitrary extra constant.
        Found via the fuzzer: A↑B with 8 independently huge-magnitude
        (near int64_max) int elements in A crashed the interpreter
        outright (SEGV in Bif_F12_TAKE::fill, via a wrapped-negative
        volume that slipped past Value::init_ravel()'s "small value"
        fast path and got a too-small buffer); A⍴B with the same shape
        of input didn't crash but silently produced a corrupted Value
        (⍴ and ≢ report the bogus huge shape, but indexing it throws
        INDEX ERROR -- worse than a crash, since it looks like it
        worked). Both rows used the exact same unbounded raw A_stim
        before this fix. Root cause: Shape::add_shape_item()'s
        "volume *= len" has no overflow check, by design -- it's on a
        hot path, and the maintainer's call was that generators must
        never hand it values whose product can overflow, rather than
        paying for a check there.
 rval.def's A_stim field is preprocessor-tokenized (unquoted), so it
 cannot hold an arbitrary APL expression -- MARKER is a bare ASCII
 string instead, and this function does the real work in real APL.
»»»

∇Z←MARKER CONFORM_A B;RB;OV;LASTAX;N;EXTRA;I;SHP
 RB←⍴⍴B
 →(MARKER≡'~BL')/LASTVARY
 →(MARKER≡'~BF')/FIRSTVARY
 →(MARKER≡'~BC')/COUNTVARY
 →(MARKER≡'~BE')/EXPANDVARY
 →(MARKER≡'~BR')/RANKVARY
 →(MARKER≡'~BD')/DROPVARY
 →(MARKER≡'~BDF')/DROPVARYFIRST
 →(MARKER≡'~S')/INTSCALAR
 →(MARKER≡'~BP')/PERMVARY
 →(MARKER≡'~BI')/INDEXVARY
 →(MARKER≡'~BCF')/COUNTVARYFIRST
 →(MARKER≡'~BEF')/EXPANDVARYFIRST
 →(MARKER≡'~BDEC')/DECODEVARY
 →(MARKER≡'~BMM')/MATMULVARY
 →(MARKER≡'~BDOM')/DOMINOVARY
 →(MARKER≡'~FMT')/FMTVARY
 →(MARKER≡'~BIDX')/BIDXVARY
 →(MARKER≡'~BPICK')/PICKVARY
 →(MARKER≡'~BIJ')/BIJVARY
 →(MARKER≡'~SC')/SHAPECNTVARY
 →(MARKER≡'~SCR')/SHAPECNTVARYR
 →(MARKER≡'~BGRADE')/GRADEVARY
 Z←⍬ ◊ →0                     ⍝ unknown marker (shouldn't happen)
LASTVARY:
 →(0=RB)/SCALARFORM
 ⊣««« ⍝ ecount here must be the EXACT volume of the shape being
  requested, not the blanket CONTEXT.BUDGET: passing CONTEXT.BUDGET as
  ecount lets do_eval_B's own clipping shrink the LAST axis of THIS
  shape whenever its volume exceeds budget (common once B itself is
  sizeable, since A must match B's other axes exactly) -- silently
  breaking the exact-shape-match this marker exists to guarantee, and
  producing a LENGTH ERROR downstream (found via a real fuzzer sweep
  on A,B/A⍪B/A⌽B/A⊖B).
 »»»
 SHP←(¯1↓⍴B),¯1+?101
 OV←RB SHP (1 1 1 1 1) 3 (×/SHP)
 →DONE
FIRSTVARY:
 →(0=RB)/SCALARFORM
 SHP←(¯1+?101),1↓⍴B
 OV←RB SHP (1 1 1 1 1) 3 (×/SHP)
 →DONE
COUNTVARY:
 ⊣««« ⍝ No 1⌈ clamp here: every ~BC/~BE/~BCF/~BEF caller's B_stim gives
  rank 0 (scalar) a weight of 0, so B is never scalar for these rows --
  the old "1⌈...(or 1 if B scalar)" fallback was dead code for its
  stated purpose and instead miscorrected the different, genuinely
  valid case of B's last axis being 0 (an empty B), generating A with
  1 element/1-count when B actually needed 0, i.e. a guaranteed LENGTH
  ERROR (found via a real fuzzer sweep: A/B, A\B, A⌿B, A⍀B, A⊂B,
  A⊂[X]B, A/[X]B, A\[X]B all showed high LENGTH ERROR rates from this).

  Conform to axis X, not always the last axis, when X is already known:
  ONE_PRIMITIVE now derives X before calling CONFORM_A (X is visible
  here via dynamic scoping, unlocalized), so for the [X] variants
  (A/[X]B etc.) this conforms to whichever axis X actually turned out
  to be, instead of blindly assuming the last one -- correct only by
  luck before this fix (found the same fuzzer sweep: A/[X]B, A\[X]B,
  A⊂[X]B all showed ~30% LENGTH ERROR from exactly this mismatch).
 »»»
 →(0<≢X)/COUNTVARY_X
 LASTAX←¯1↑⍴B                  ⍝ no axis known: conform to B's last axis
 →COUNTVARY_GO
COUNTVARY_X:
 LASTAX←(⍴B)[X]                ⍝ axis X already known: conform to THAT axis
COUNTVARY_GO:
 OV←1 LASTAX (0 1 0 0 0) 0 CONTEXT.BUDGET
 Z←4|⎕RVAL OV                  ⍝ ⎕RVAL ints are unbounded magnitude and
 →0                            ⍝ signed; 4| forces 0..3, non-negative
EXPANDVARY:
 →(0<≢X)/EXPANDVARY_X
 LASTAX←¯1↑⍴B
 →EXPANDVARY_GO
EXPANDVARY_X:
 LASTAX←(⍴B)[X]
EXPANDVARY_GO:
 EXTRA←(?11)-1                 ⍝ 0..10 extra zero-fill slots
 N←LASTAX+EXTRA
 Z←(LASTAX⍴1),EXTRA⍴0
 Z←Z[N?N]                      ⍝ shuffle so the 1s land at random spots
 →0
RANKVARY:
 ⍝ int only, even for scalar B (RB=0) -- unlike LASTVARY/FIRSTVARY's
 ⍝ shared SCALARFORM fallback (which deliberately allows any type for
 ⍝ catenate/rotate-family A), ~BR feeds a drop/take-count and must
 ⍝ never be char/float/complex/nested (confirmed via fuzzer: A↓B with
 ⍝ scalar B was generating a character A, an immediate DOMAIN ERROR).
 →(0≠RB)/RANKVARY_GO ⋄ RB←1
RANKVARY_GO:
 OV←1 RB (0 1 0 0 0) 0 CONTEXT.BUDGET
 Z←⎕RVAL OV
 →0
SHAPECNTVARYR:
 ⍝ ~SCR: A↑B's take-count vector. Same ⍴⍴B-length rule and same
 ⍝ scalar-B (RB=0 -> length 1) handling as RANKVARY (~BR, A↓B's
 ⍝ marker) above, plus DROPVARY's 1000| magnitude cap -- unlike ~BR,
 ⍝ an uncapped huge take count is exactly the crash the ~SC history
 ⍝ documents (see the ~SC comment above).
 →(0≠RB)/SHAPECNTVARYR_GO ⋄ RB←1
SHAPECNTVARYR_GO:
 OV←1 RB (0 1 0 0 0) 0 CONTEXT.BUDGET
 Z←1000|⎕RVAL OV
 →0
DROPVARY:
 →(RB≤1)/INTSCALAR              ⍝ RB=0: scalar; RB=1: no leading axes left
 SHP←¯1↓⍴B                      ⍝ exact-volume ecount -- see LASTVARY comment
 OV←(RB-1) SHP (0 1 0 0 0) 0 (×/SHP)
 Z←1000|⎕RVAL OV                ⍝ cap magnitude: GNU APL's ⌽/⊖ are O(N)
 →0                             ⍝ in the rotate amount, huge values hang
DROPVARYFIRST:
 →(RB≤1)/INTSCALAR              ⍝ RB=0: scalar; RB=1: no trailing axes left
 SHP←1↓⍴B
 OV←(RB-1) SHP (0 1 0 0 0) 0 (×/SHP)
 Z←1000|⎕RVAL OV
 →0
INTSCALAR:
 OV←0 ⍬ (0 1 0 0 0) 0 CONTEXT.BUDGET
 Z←1000|⎕RVAL OV
 →0
PERMVARY:
 ⍝ A⍉B requires A to be *dense*: every integer 1..(⌈/A) must occur at
 ⍝ least once (each output axis needs at least one source axis), not
 ⍝ just "each element independently 1..RB" as this used to generate --
 ⍝ confirmed directly via reproducer (A←2 3 3 for a rank-3 B DOMAIN
 ⍝ ERRORs with "axis 1 is missing in A", since 1 never got drawn).
 ⍝ RB?RB (Deal) gives a true random permutation of 1..RB, which is
 ⍝ trivially dense (a bijection).
 →(0=RB)/EMPTYFORM
 Z←RB?RB
 →0
INDEXVARY:
 →(0=RB)/EMPTYFORM
 OV←1 RB (0 1 0 0 0) 0 CONTEXT.BUDGET
 Z←1+(⍴B)|(⎕RVAL OV)-1           ⍝ element i forced into 1..(⍴B)[i]
 →0
EMPTYFORM:
 Z←⍬
 →0
COUNTVARYFIRST:
 ⊣««« ⍝ (⍴B)[1], not the "1↑⍴B,1" append trick: that trick only gives
  B's first axis when B has rank ≥ 2 (appending a scalar there extends
  the LAST axis by one slice, leaving the first axis untouched). For
  rank-1 B -- which does happen here, B_stim gives rank 1 nonzero
  weight -- appending a scalar to a plain vector just makes it one
  element longer, so the trick silently returned B's length+1 instead
  of its (only, and therefore also first) axis length. (⍴B)[1] is
  correct regardless of rank, since B is never scalar here (so ⍴B
  always has ≥1 element).
 »»»
 LASTAX←(⍴B)[1]                 ⍝ B's first axis size
 OV←1 LASTAX (0 1 0 0 0) 0 CONTEXT.BUDGET
 Z←4|⎕RVAL OV
 →0
EXPANDVARYFIRST:
 LASTAX←(⍴B)[1]
 EXTRA←(?11)-1
 N←LASTAX+EXTRA
 Z←(LASTAX⍴1),EXTRA⍴0
 Z←Z[N?N]
 →0
DECODEVARY:
 ⊣««« ⍝ Same two bugs as COUNTVARYFIRST/LASTVARY had (see their comments):
  "1↑⍴B,1" only extracts B's first axis correctly when B has rank ≥ 2
  (A⊥B's B_stim allows rank 1, where the append trick instead returns
  B's length+1); and CONTEXT.BUDGET as ecount lets do_eval_B clip the
  requested shape's last axis whenever its volume exceeds budget,
  corrupting the exact match this marker exists to guarantee. (⍴B)[1]
  and an exact-volume ecount fix both, matching the same fix already
  applied to COUNTVARYFIRST/LASTVARY/DROPVARY/MATMULVARY/DOMINOVARY.
  This was very likely the direct cause of a real A⊥B segfault: the
  reproducer had B of shape 0 76 (first axis 0), which the old 1⌈
  clamp would have forced LASTAX to 1 instead, generating an A that
  did not actually match B's real (empty) first axis.
 »»»
 LASTAX←(⍴B)[1]                 ⍝ B's first axis size (B is never scalar here)
 OV←1 LASTAX (0 1 1 0 0) 0 LASTAX  ⍝ rank 1, shape LASTAX, ecount = volume = LASTAX
 Z←⎕RVAL OV
 →0
MATMULVARY:
 LASTAX←(⍴B)[1]                 ⍝ B's row count (first axis); B is always rank 2
 SHP←(¯1+?101),LASTAX
 ⍝ complex excluded: f (inner product's reduce step) can be ⌈/⌊, which
 ⍝ have no total order on complex numbers -- A's type mask was
 ⍝ hardcoded here independently of B's own (already-fixed) mask, so
 ⍝ Af.gB kept generating complex A even after B's mask was corrected.
 OV←2 SHP (0 1 1 0 0) 0 (×/SHP)
 Z←⎕RVAL OV
 →0
DOMINOVARY:
 ⊣««« ⍝ A⌹B (dyadic Domino) has the same "rows ≥ columns" requirement on
  B as monadic ⌹B (see rval.def), so A⌹B's arity conditionally
  transposes B whenever rows < columns, exactly like ⌹B's arity does.
  A must then conform to B's EFFECTIVE (post-transpose) row count, not
  its raw first-axis length -- ⌈/⍴B (the larger of the two axes) is
  exactly that, since transposing (when it happens) always swaps to the
  larger axis first.
 »»»
 LASTAX←⌈/⍴B                    ⍝ B's effective (post-transpose) row count
 →((?2)=1)/DOMINOVEC
 SHP←LASTAX,¯1+?101
 OV←2 SHP (0 1 1 0 0) 0 (×/SHP)
 Z←⎕RVAL OV
 →0
DOMINOVEC:
 OV←1(,LASTAX)(0 1 1 0 0) 0 LASTAX
 Z←⎕RVAL OV
 →0
FMTVARY:
 N←?2                          ⍝ length 1 or 2 (⎕IO=1: ?2 is already 1 or 2)
 OV←1(,N)(0 1 0 0 0) 0 CONTEXT.BUDGET
 Z←21|⎕RVAL OV                 ⍝ non-negative, small (0..20)
 ⍝ N=1: Z alone is DECIMALS (auto width, always safe). N=2: Z[1] is
 ⍝ WIDTH, Z[2] DECIMALS -- an independently-random WIDTH is almost
 ⍝ always too narrow to hold B's integer part + '.' + DECIMALS digits
 ⍝ (confirmed directly: 3 5⍕0.56... and even 22 20⍕<10-digit int>.5
 ⍝ both DOMAIN ERROR), so force WIDTH to 0 (auto) instead of leaving
 ⍝ it to chance.
 →(N=1)/FMTDONE ⋄ Z[1]←0
FMTDONE:
 →0
BIDXVARY:
 N←1⌈≢B                        ⍝ ≢B is B's own tally (works for scalar too)
 OV←¯2 ¯5 (0 1 0 0 0) 0 CONTEXT.BUDGET
 Z←1+N|(⎕RVAL OV)-1            ⍝ every element forced into 1..≢B
 →0
PICKVARY:
 →(0=RB)/EMPTYFORM             ⍝ scalar B: A must be ⍬
 →(1=RB)/PICKSCALAR            ⍝ vector B: A is a plain scalar index
 OV←1 RB (0 1 0 0 0) 0 CONTEXT.BUDGET
 Z←⊂1+(⍴B)|(⎕RVAL OV)-1        ⍝ rank≥2: one enclosed RB-element index
 →0                            ⍝ path, element i forced into 1..(⍴B)[i]
PICKSCALAR:
 OV←0 ⍬ (0 1 0 0 0) 0 CONTEXT.BUDGET
 Z←1+(≢B)|(⎕RVAL OV)-1         ⍝ scalar index forced into 1..≢B
 →0
BIJVARY:
 Z←⍬ ◊ I←0
BIJLOOP:
 I←I+1
 →(I>RB)/0                     ⍝ Z now has RB nested per-axis index vectors
 N←(?11)-1                     ⍝ this axis's index-vector length, 0..10
 OV←1(,N)(0 1 0 0 0) 0 CONTEXT.BUDGET
 Z←Z,⊂1+((⍴B)[I])|(⎕RVAL OV)-1 ⍝ values forced into 1..(⍴B)[I]
 →BIJLOOP
GRADEVARY:
 ⍝ dyadic A⍋B / A⍒B: A is a vector of *distinct* characters defining a
 ⍝ custom collating sequence, and every character in B must appear in
 ⍝ A (else DOMAIN ERROR -- confirmed directly: 'cba'⍋B fails whenever B
 ⍝ contains a char outside 'cba'). A generated independently of B
 ⍝ almost never covers B's actual characters, so build A from B's own
 ⍝ distinct characters instead, shuffled into a random order.
 Z←∪,B
 →(0=≢Z)/0                     ⍝ B has no characters at all: Z stays ⍬
 Z←Z[(≢Z)?≢Z]
 →0
SHAPECNTVARY:
 ⍝ generate a THROWAWAY array and take its OWN shape as Z, instead of
 ⍝ generating up to 8 magnitude-capped ints independently: ⎕RVAL already
 ⍝ clips a generated array's shape internally so ×/(that shape) ≤
 ⍝ CONTEXT.BUDGET (see Quad_RVAL.cc's do_eval_B), and since ×/R ≥ ⌈/R
 ⍝ for non-negative R, that ×/⍴Z≤budget bound also bounds every
 ⍝ individual axis length in Z -- no separate per-element cap needed,
 ⍝ and unlike the discarded 100| cap this ties the guarantee to the
 ⍝ same budget the rest of the fuzzer already respects.
 OV←¯8 ¯1000 (0 1 0 0 0) 0 CONTEXT.BUDGET
 Z←⍴⎕RVAL OV
 →0
SCALARFORM:
 OV←0 ⍬ (1 1 1 1 0) 0 CONTEXT.BUDGET
DONE:
 Z←⎕RVAL OV
∇

      ⊣««« ⍝ CONTEXT is a set of parameters that govern the testcase
       execution in all sub-functions. To be adapted by the user
       as needed. Uses structured variables (a GNU APL extension).
      »»»

      CONTEXT.BUDGET←10000        ⍝ desired ⍴∈ limit, see 5 ⎕RVAL B
      CONTEXT.TD ← 15 70 15 5 5   ⍝ type distribution: char, int,
                                  ⍝ double, complex, nested (in %)
      CONTEXT.SECONDS←5           ⍝ how long ALL_PRIMITIVES fuzzes
      ⊣««« ⍝ CONTEXT.FPOOL: scalar dyadic (also valid monadic) functions
       that substitute for the 'f'/'g' placeholders used by
       operator-taking primitives in the ⎕RVAL fuzz table
       (e.g. f/, f¨, f.g).
      »»»

      CONTEXT.FPOOL←'+-×⌈⌊'

      ALL_PRIMITIVES
1

      )ERASE CONTEXT

      )CHECK BRIEF

